The Problem: The "Spaghetti Code" Web

Imagine you are building a smart airport system. You have four classes:
    1. Airplane
    2. Runway
    3. Helicopter
    4. CargoTruck

The Beginner Way:
If an Airplane is landing, it has to tell the Runway it is coming. But it also has to tell the Helicopter to stay away,
and it has to tell the CargoTruck to wait.
Suddenly, every single object has to hold a reference to every other object in the system.

If you draw lines between all these objects, it looks like a spiderweb. This is called Tightly Coupled Spaghetti Code.
If you delete the Helicopter class, the Airplane class breaks because it had a hardcoded reference to it!
The Senior Solution: The Mediator (The Air Traffic Controller)

The Mediator Pattern says: "Objects are no longer allowed to talk to each other directly."

Instead, we create a central hub—a Mediator. (Think of the Air Traffic Control tower).
If the Airplane wants to land, it doesn't talk to the Helicopter. The Airplane sends a message to the Tower: "I am
landing." The Tower is the only object that knows who everyone else is, and the Tower broadcasts the message to the
Helicopter and the Cargo Truck.